WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:10.180 align:start position:0%
 
we all make mistakes  you've probably heard these words before  usually their words people say when  they're trying to make you feel better  you've just said something a little  stupid maybe you tried to pay someone a  compliment and it came out really  awkward and now you're worried that you  might have offended them maybe you  carelessly gave away a secret that  wasn't yours to tell or maybe you lost  your temper and you said something you  didn't really mean something you regret  and now you're feeling guilty or  embarrassed and one of your friends  tries to comfort you by reminding you  that we've all been there don't worry  about it she says nobody is perfect we  all make mistakes and maybe hearing this  makes you feel better because it's true  nobody's perfect we all go around  talking to people all day every day and  every once in a while we're bound to say  the wrong thing no big deal but what if  our friend is wrong what if it is a big  deal what if our words actually matter  in today's reading from the New  Testament James starts his discussion of  the power of language by reminding us  that nobody's perfect all of us make  many mistakes he says but James is not  telling us this to comfort us he's  trying to make us uncomfortable he's  trying to make us think about our  tongues not as harmless floppy pink  flaps covered in taste buds but as  deadly instruments of devastating power  the tongue is a fire he tells us it is a  restless evil full of deadly poison  according to James our tongue steers our  whole body it guides our movement  through the world like the rudder of a  ship and if we let our tongues run away  with us we drift off course we poison  our friendships we start a forest fire  that we can't put out the very same  tongue that we use to praise God can be  used to curse people who are made in  God's image our words are like water  from a spring bubbling up deep within us  cool and refreshing or salty and  stinging our words are the sweet or  bitter fruit that we bear God use words  to speak the world into being God said  let there be light and there was light  but words can also plunge us into  darkness  I was thinking about the destructive  power of words last week during a long  conversation with one of my little  sisters when she was in elementary  school my brother and I were in high  school and to this day my little sister  vividly remembers how it felt to be  sitting at the dinner table every night  as my brother and I talked on and on  about all the big kids stuff that was  important to us and as a little kid she  could barely get a word in edgewise she  would start to say something that  mattered to her from her seven-year-old  perspective and we big kids would talk  over her or interrupt her or tease her  sometimes we laughed at her we didn't  really listen and for years she felt  frustrated and diminished and silenced  and as long as my sister and I live  whenever we talk to each other she will  remember somewhere in her soul what it  felt like to grow up feeling drowned out  by the sound of her siblings voices our  overwhelming words were a threat to her  developing sense of self and 20 years  later that hurt is still going  it can be painful and scary to think  about the damage that our words can do  especially if we're not sure how it can  be undone but James wants us to think  about it he wants us to confront the  power of our own language because he  knows that this power can be harnessed  for good God wants us to learn to  harness it for good but how the first  step is to stop thinking about our words  as less important than our actions and  to start thinking about words as one of  the most important things that we do  about 60 years ago an Oxford philosopher  named Jael Austin developed a new theory  of language instead of thinking about  speech and action as distinct things  where action is doing things and speech  is just talking about things Austin  reconceptualized speech as a kind of  action he developed a concept he called  speech acts statements that actually  make things happen in the real world and  he wrote a book called how to do things  with words that talked about how certain  kinds of sentences actually change the  world around us just by virtue of being  said a parent names a baby and that name  becomes the word that will follow her  all the days of her life a minister says  I now pronounce you wife and wife and  two women began a marriage together that  will last until one of them dies Jael  Austin didn't necessarily believe that  all speech was action but in a way it is  and James certainly thinks it is  thinking about our speech as real  consequential action is a first step in  learning how to wield our voices so much  of the time when we think that there's  nothing we can do in a situation there's  something we can say  and if we're thoughtful and creative and  a tune to God's Spirit we can discover a  way to say it I'm a writing teacher so I  spent a lot of my time thinking about  language and how it works and how it can  work better I'm always scribbling on  students drafts saying things like great  introduction or good use of imagery or  this sentence has no verb sometimes I  say I do not think that this word means  what you think it means lately in  English 120 I've been talking with my  students about how writers develop a  sense of voice how is it that we read a  few sentences from Joan Didion or James  Baldwin and we know instantly who is  talking to us and how can we as writers  develop a voice that is clear and  beautiful and distinctively our own how  can we choose words that draw our  readers in and give them pleasure and  leave them with the gift of new meaning  managing words on the page is a very  different thing than forming words with  our lungs and vocal cords and sending  them into the air but I think there are  some real similarities we all have these  god-given voices that we can use to say  anything and through thoughtful choices  and creativity and sensitivity to the  people we're in conversation with we can  develop our voices into the sound of  blessing yes we all make many mistakes  but we can all do a lot of good as well  our words are like a long poem dedicated  to God dedicated to each other our words  are a spoken word performance part  planned part improvised bubbling out of  the deep Brooks that's well within our  souls  and as we talk about our life and we  talk to the people in it we are helping  to tell our own story and the story of  those around us and the story of the God  who surrounds us all the God who spoke  us all into being who named us before  the foundations of the world and God has  called us to share in this work of  naming and storytelling and composing  poems of praise the creative and  consequential work of everyday  conversation and daily speech to put a  new spin on an old phrase if we want to  follow Jesus we can't just walk the walk  we have to talk the talk so think about  your tongue for a minute feel it sitting  there warm and alive in your mouth  sheath behind your teeth ready and  waiting it's going to get quite a  workout this week it's going to say all  kinds of things and the speech X it  performs can be sharp and shattering  like an axe or solid and strong and  constructive like bricks and mortar or  soft and cool like aloe vera lotion on a  sunburn what do you want to say this  week what things do you want to do with  your words  sometimes if you say yes to something  that you know is good but you're still a  little scared of your tongue can lead  you boldly into new adventures and  sometimes if you say notice something  that's wrong or unnecessary your tongue  can protect you or someone else right  now there is someone in your life to  whom you can say I love you a friend a  family member God and when you say it  their heart will begin to glow there is  someone in your life to whom you can say  I'm sorry or I forgive you and their  heart might begin to melt there is  someone in your life to whom you can say  I need some help and they will help you  and there is someone in your life you  don't even know yet who is going to be  one of your best friends who might just  need you to say hi this week say  something that you've been needing to  say for a long time weigh your words on  your tongue feel their power choose them  carefully and then send them out into  the world to do their work if you are  ever tongue-tied then you can sit in  silence and really listen to someone  else that's often the best gift you can  give the most loving thing that you can  do  and if you were ever alone with lots to  say but no one to say it to then pour  out your soul and complaint and  gratitude and petition and praise to god  I'll close with a short New Year's  prayer that someone taught me a long  time ago it's a good prayer for the  beginning of a new school year let us  pray  Jesus you are the first word I utter in  the new year may your name forever be  laughter in my mouth and when I come to  die may Jesus also be my last word amen

